## Proof

We evaluate
$$L=\lim_{x\to\infty}\left(\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt\right)^{1/x^2}.$$

**Step 1: Asymptotic of the integral.** We claim
$$\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt \sim \frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\quad(x\to\infty).$$

Split $\int_0^x = \int_0^1 + \int_1^x$; the first piece is a constant, so it suffices to handle $\int_1^x$. Integrate by parts with $u=\frac{1}{2t}$, $dv=2t\,e^{t^2}\,dt$ (so $v=e^{t^2}$):
$$\int_1^x e^{t^2}\,dt=\left[\frac{e^{t^2}}{2t}\right]_1^x+\int_1^x\frac{e^{t^2}}{2t^2}\,dt=\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}-\frac{e}{2}+\int_1^x\frac{e^{t^2}}{2t^2}\,dt.$$

It remains to show the remainder is $o\!\left(\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\right)$. By L'Hôpital,
$$\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\int_1^x \frac{e^{t^2}}{2t^2}\,dt}{\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}}=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x^2}}{\frac{d}{dx}\!\left(\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\right)}.$$
Since $\frac{d}{dx}\!\left(\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\right)=e^{x^2}\!\left(1-\frac{1}{2x^2}\right)\sim e^{x^2}$, the ratio tends to
$$\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{e^{x^2}/(2x^2)}{e^{x^2}}=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{1}{2x^2}=0.$$
Hence $\int_1^x e^{t^2}\,dt\sim\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}$, and adding the constant $\int_0^1 e^{t^2}\,dt$ gives
$$\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt=\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\,(1+o(1)).$$

**Step 2: Compute the limit.** Taking logarithms,
$$\ln L=\lim_{x\to\infty}\frac{1}{x^2}\ln\!\left(\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt\right).$$
Using the asymptotic,
$$\ln\!\left(\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt\right)=\ln\!\left(\frac{e^{x^2}}{2x}\right)+\ln(1+o(1))=x^2-\ln(2x)+o(1).$$
Therefore
$$\frac{1}{x^2}\ln\!\left(\int_0^x e^{t^2}\,dt\right)=1-\frac{\ln(2x)}{x^2}+\frac{o(1)}{x^2}\xrightarrow[x\to\infty]{}1,$$
since $\frac{\ln(2x)}{x^2}\to 0$. Thus $\ln L=1$, i.e.
$$\boxed{L=e}.$$

